[[ch11-handbook-direct-trade]]
== Handbook: Direct Trade

While these chapters are intended to be read independently, this chapter does assume an MGO of fixed size thirty people as detailed in the chapter, "The Fellowship of The Ring".

So, your fellowship of the ring of trust is established. You have an MGO of fixed size thirty people. The first logical quest is direct trade.

Why trade? Well, from what I've read of history the thirteen US colonies were all successful in their own way in trade before the revolutionary war. The colonies set up a trade triangle of whiskey, tobacco and slaves. Lumber, sugar, and other products were also heavily traded. Trade was the foundation that Britain wanted from the colonies and the colonies excelled at it.

Trade is the logical first step for establishing New Democracy. So the first quest after forming your MGO, your fellowship, is to establish trade. You may not know this but capitalism didn't exist in the colonies prior to the Revolutionary war. Taxes did, of course. Taxes lead to the Revolutionary war. So the quest will be to establish direct trade without taxes. Direct trade doesn't involve public currency. This means no capitalism and no taxes.

Quests are statements of objectives and not statements of how to go about them. The quest is to establish direct trade within the MGO where direct trade means no currency, no capitalism and no taxes. For example, garage sales are popular in the US. Garage sales are a way to direct trade for small amounts of cash that one typically isn't reported for tax collection. Your MGO could establish garage sales monthly for just members of the MGO. Why just MGO members? Because there are two objectives of this quest. One is to establish direct trade but the second is to continue to practice building trust. Your quest, adventurer, is to establish direct trade that builds trust within the MGO. 

The rest of this chapter is food for thought. Examples of ways your MGO may go about this.

Flea markets are a form of garage sale. Are they direct trade? Well, if one makes more than a fist full of dollars then you probably want to report that for taxes. Is there a way to hold a flea market without paying taxes? Yes. Direct trade of item exchange. I give you a power drill, you give me a coffee table. Let's call this an exchange flea market. Say, once a month your MGO members meet in some yard and bring items to trade.

The valuable aspect of garage sales and exchange flea markets is that no computer software is needed. People just talk to each other and learn to barter. I can just hear people in their mind thinking, "but why do this physically? Why not use some computer software like a spreadsheet?" Well, the quest includes learning to trust people. In addition, any time computers are involved then the trust is biased to those more comfortable using computers.

Potluck Markets build on exchange flea markets. Again the notion here is to build MGO community trust. So, don't just have a garage sale or exchange flea market sale, have a potluck market sale! One could have variations on this such as a Picnic Market. Or perhaps a Street Festival market! Add some live music. We need to rebuild our societies and what better way than food and music! One can still maintain the direct trade without money and do pretty much anything done today as far as trading used items.

The importance of live direct trade events to build trust cannot be overstated.

However, of course we'll be wanting to use computers.

Perpetual market. The first computer market is the perpetual market. A perpetual market is simply listing all for exchange items in some software application in a permanent inventory. This software doesn't exist today. The idea is simple. Just list items for exchange in a permanent inventory per MGO user. The benefit here is people within the MGO can view exchange items 24/7. So, how is the trade initiated? Easy? Remember this is limited to the MGO of thirty people. The MGO should already have a directory of MGO members with phone numbers, or Discord server, or Slack server, or MS Teams server, or whatever communications technology is being used. Just talk to the owner directly.

IOU market. An IOU was a marker people used when currency wasn't available. An IOU was a pieced of paper listing what was owed. In this case an IOU market would be for intangible things like labor. Say, for example, one offers to trade labor of mowing someone's yard for three months in exchange for a coffee table. The IOU computer software would allow the labor items to be listed and further tallied as the labor is provided. The IOU market works well with a perpetual market in that the ongoing tally of labor items can be tracked.

Trust side note. There is another chapter on MGO suits, but it is worth pointing out that people are always going to sue over even the smallest perceived slights. For example, say a power drill was traded and it breaks on first use. The power drill owner wants some kind of refund even though it was traded for as is. Your MGO should already have established some trust resolution process. This is a key part of learning to trust. This is also why the direct trade quest should be limited to just those in the MGO. This way the conflict resolutions can apply.

Bulk market. Pooling resources is a core benefit to forming an MGO corporation. The MGO can buy say Internet service as a corporation and then distribute that to every MGO member much cheaper than if buying the Internet service individually. However, the bulk market distributes to only a subset of the MGO members leaving some members perhaps wanting to trade. Say the MGO buys 25lb. bags of rice from the corporate cash. How to direct trade not using money?  One idea is an open bar. This is similar to how corporations provide free soda, tea and breakfast foods in a break room. In this case perhaps sundry bulk items are stocked such that everyone benefits from something in the open bar. Or, an IOU market could track how much bulk items are used by MGO members and then some future item or labor exchange is agreed upon.

MGO community direct trade. As MGOs become more common, MGOs can build on their internal trust skills using internal direct trade to branch out to other MGOs. When MGOs trade they trade as MGO units, not as individual members. Further, MGO community networks should cap their MGO network to size thirty. Thirty times thirty is nine-hundred people. That's a fairly large group. But, if that thirty size network of thirty MGOs happens then the same concept applies. These groups of 30 would network with other groups of thirty.


Private currency. Bitcoin is literally nothing special. It is just private currency. Private currency is not allowed for public sales. Private currency builds on a bulk market. Anyone who has worked in a somewhat large corporation are familiar with corporate shops that sell swag. The corporate swag is usually listed with two prices. One price is in dollars and the other is in corporate private currency. A common case of this is holiday gifts. Corporations commonly give out say $200 corporate dollars. An employee can though go through the corporate swag shop and buy a mug or other corporate gear using the gifted holiday corporate private currency dollars. The idea here is similar. For example, say every member of an MGO is given $100 MGO dollars. Further bulk items are broken up into smaller items of sale for less than $100. Direct trade can be augmented with corporate private currency items. Say I direct trade my power drill for your coffee table and $2 MGO. Private currency augments bulk markets by offering common items at fixed private currency prices in order to allow for granularity.

Everything presented here is direct trade and is public currency free. No public money means no taxes. 











